This case is set up to run the Sydney Inhomogeneous Burner in the Lr300-v59
configuration. In that configuration, the fuel inlet is nearly premixed. The
case runs with a 2D manifold (ZMIX, PROG) based on nonpremixed counterflow
flamelets. Z=1 is set to correspond to phi=4.6 (the mean equivalence ratio from
the mixing tube) rather than pure CH4. In this case, mixture fraction
fluctuations in the fuel inlet stream are neglected and it is just set to Z=1.

The case can be run with up to 5 levels of refinement.  I'd recommend starting
with 3 levels (which fits well on a single Kestrel node), then increasing
after the flow develops a bit and running on more nodes. The case is set up
for a combination of static and adaptive refinement. 2D plane data is saved
at axial locations corresponding to where experimental measurements were made.

Once the case is run, statistics can be computed and compared to experiment.
In the generated figures, black is mean and red is RMS. Lines are the
simulation, dots are experiment. The command below will average data from
all 2D plt files matching the path provided and compare to the appropriate
experimental data. The command can be repeated for each zoverD. 

     python radial_stats.py -f "stats/zoverD001/plt001*0" -s

Not included in this repo, but important files are located on Ketsrel at

    /projects/safvto/bruce/sydney_new/data

These files include the experimental data (data2015 and data5GP, the latter
of which is the "better" data set). There are several chemtable files
for various modeling approaches. The one used here is `sydney_2D_xf03333.ctb`.
There are three turbulent inflow files, for the Lr300_v59, Lr75_v57, Lr75_v80
cases as indicated. These files contain the velocity, mixture fraction
(defined with Z=1 being pure CH4), and mixture fraction squared (used to
compute mixture fraction variance), at a plane 1 diameter upstream
of the nozzle exit plane. 